    {
      "id": "rath-yatra",
      "topic": "culture",
      "title": "Rath Yatra",
      "body": "Every summer in Puri, the images of Jagannath and his siblings are pulled through the streets on huge wooden chariots, rebuilt each year. The festival, Rath Yatra, draws hundreds of thousands of pilgrims; the English word “juggernaut” comes from Jagannath's chariot.",
      "tags": [
        "culture",
        "festivals",
        "Puri"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sounds",
      "topic": "sounds",
      "title": "Sounds to watch",
      "body": "Odia keeps short and long vowels and the dental / retroflex contrast (ତ / ଟ, ଦ / ଡ). The inherent vowel is a rounded “ô”, closer to “o” than to Hindi’s “a”, so କ is read kô.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "ତା · ଟା",
          "r": "tā · ṭā",
          "e": "dental t vs retroflex ṭ"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "pronunciation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "politeness",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "ତୁ and ଆପଣ",
      "body": "ତୁ (tu) is intimate “you”; ତୁମେ (tume) is familiar; ଆପଣ (āpaṇa) is polite. Verb endings follow the level, as in other eastern Indo-Aryan languages.",
